A||N||11||FieroMaster88||DOH!!!!!!  ||||8||
Z||000000||FieroMaster88||12-19-2000||01:19 AM||||Well, I purchased my 88 coupe in mid October with 27,000 miles on it.   It cost me $4000, but I needed a car and would NOT buy anything but a Fiero and would not pass up that deal <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">.....so I spent the money....Not thinking about winter.  So now here I am in Michigan driving this mint condition 88 coupe in the snow and ice, with the salt and stupid drivers every day because I cant afford another car!!!!!!!  THIS TOTALY SUCKS!!!  I wash the darn thing like 3 times a week (seriously) and scrub the underside.  To show u how crazy this is, I washed it today, and I dont even think that the temperature made it to 20!  And of course, this is one of the worst winters we have had in years!!!!!!  DOH!! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">  <P>Today i pulled it into the shop at school (I am studdying to become a mechenic) to do a full service oil change and fully detailed the underside...Thankfully there is still no rust on ANYTHING!!!  The only problems I am having is that the Pontiac emblem on the rear bumper has some of the silver paint peeling off, my fiero emblem on the front facia came unglued (i still have it, too cold to reglue), and I am worried about the paint.<P>I was wondering if anyone else out there is driving such a rare Fiero in these conditions or if I am just stupid!!!!!  Also, will the ice and all effect the paint job????  How can I prevent scratches...Because I cant afford another car!! <P>Thanks,<P>James Essar||||reg||8
Z||000001||pHoOl||12-19-2000||01:31 AM||||I drive an 88GT, and i'm in the Detroit area right now.  Yeah, nice weather we're having.<P>Story for ya,<P>i was driving today on my way to give blood at my church.  pull in to the subdivision and the back end swings a little around the curve.  i go to correct, it swings to the other side.  go to correct again, and the front end just keeps goign the same direction.  hit a snow bank, and did a 360.  threw it in park, looked at the car.  NO DAMAGE!!!!  go me.<P>anyway, thought i'd share.<BR><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.gvsuphool.homestead.com/files/pHoOl_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||FieroMaster88||12-19-2000||01:38 AM||||Yeah, <P>I did the same thing after it started snowing for the first time.  It was with my old, wore out tires.  And I was spining towards a tree and a mail box.  Luckily I missed both.....I just hate to drive my poor car!<P>James||||reg||1
Z||000003||WisconsinGT||12-19-2000||02:28 AM||||I was told that if you leave your car outside during the winter that you will not have to worry about rust. Reason being that the salt will never have a chance to break down and eat into the metal. If it is inside and warm enough, the salt will break down and start to eat at the metal. I do not know if this is true or not. But it sounds good.||||reg||1
Z||000004||mrfiero||12-19-2000||02:56 AM||||I drove my 16K mile '88 GT in the snow once (it had 12K miles at the time).  I was the most paranoid driver on the road!  I drove it like a little old lady.  Luckily we don't use salt here in Colorado, but the sand we use does a number on paint jobs and windshields!<P>I think that as long as you keep washing it regularly you should be OK, but I would avoid driving it if you can help it.||||reg||1
Z||000005||WKDFIRO||12-19-2000||07:15 AM||||When we would work on Fieros from the east we would find the rust within the nuts and bolts of the suspension. Such as the control arm bolts or various bolts on the struts (holding the knuckle) and anywhere where the bottom of the car had been scraped, scratching off the factory anti rust coating. The rust hadn't ruined anything but it just made those bolts and nuts especially hard to break unless soaked with penetrating oil.<P>When washing make sure to get in those areas if you are concerned with rust! ||||reg||9
Z||000006||Tigger||12-19-2000||09:43 AM||||I'd put a "generous" coat of wax on the paint to keep the snow/ice from sticking to it and salt from attacking it.<P><P>------------------<BR>T-T-F-N, Tah Tah For Now!<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/tiggerblue2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||FieroJoe||12-19-2000||10:52 AM||||I have even heard that spraying armor all on your mudflaps and even the under carriage could help with preventing snow and salt from sticking there.<P>Washing it and waxing it right now is the most you can do if you must drive it.<P>Drive carefully!<P>------------------<BR>FieroJoe<BR>c311t@yahoo.com<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||DavidHenshaw||12-19-2000||11:56 AM||||Yes this is a bad winter even here in WVa. I am not driving my white 86 GT fastback v6 while there is any snow/ice/salt on the highway. That said I only bought this car four months ago and it had 203,000 miles on it which makes me think its been driven year round by previous owners.<P>Funny thing, shortly after buying the car I did all the brakes. For a while I thought the car had been in a flood because there was so much sand/dirt embedded in all parts of the suspension. Evidently the car had been driven through some creek because there was absolutely no evidence of water having gotten inside the car. Of course it could have been a shallow flood I guess.<P>Anyway I love this car and am glad I don't have to drive it in winter weather. My sympathy is with any who have no other ride.<P>Dave||||reg||1
Z||000009||88formula||12-19-2000||11:19 PM||||WKDFIRO you’re scaring the guy. <P>You could always take a bus during the worst storms.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||Standard||12-20-2000||01:58 AM||||Well I'm driving my 86 GT 5 speed... But it's not exactly mint, especially after my mishap earlier in the season.  But I still need it for transportation, so I only get crazy when no other cars are on the road... I lost count of how many snowbanks I've taken out already.. hehe.  Amazing how easily the front end toboggans up on the snow...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Mike -<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 153,000 miles and some busted up ground f/x  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/redface.gif"> <BR>Red 84 Sport Coupe - winter donut maker    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/asthanos.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Master_Sushi||12-20-2000||10:57 AM||||I just got my 88 about 8 months ago. Im driving it all winter. Its really not "that" bad in snow. I do have snow tires on, and that helps  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">. I'd recomend snow tires for any fiero being driven through winter. They have saved me from getting stuck and spining out more then a few times allready  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"> . The way I look at it is, if I save one fender bender (or worse) this winter they basicly paid for themselfs.||||reg||10
